# Raphael backend secrets.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` beside the configuration file you pass to `raphael server serve --config`,
# or, without --config, into the directory you run `raphael server serve` from. Or set the variable in
# the server's environment directly. A variable already set in the environment always wins over this
# file, including when it is set to an empty value, which then fails startup and says so rather than
# silently falling back here.
#
# This file is an example and is committed. `.env` is ignored and must never be committed.

# The single full-access API key for this instance. Required: the server will not start without it.
#
# This is the server's variable. Clients use RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and RAPHAEL_API_KEY, set to this same
# key; the server never reads RAPHAEL_API_KEY, so a shell that starts a server can still run clients.
#
# Generate a random one:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Visible ASCII only - no spaces, control characters, or accented or non-Latin characters. That
# restriction is not cosmetic: HTTP headers carry bytes, so a key with a non-ASCII character or
# surrounding whitespace would let the server start and then fail every client, and most HTTP clients
# refuse to send one at all. Raphael refuses such a key at startup rather than running with a
# configuration that cannot work.
#
# There is no length floor, and length is not strength anyway, so generate the key rather than
# inventing one. Hex or base64url is recommended: both stay inside the accepted alphabet
# and avoid the `#` character, which a `.env` parser treats as the start of a comment in an unquoted
# value and would silently truncate. Whitespace is rejected rather than trimmed, because a credential
# is used exactly as written or not at all.
#
# Every client - mobile, web, CLI, agent - uses this one key. Rotating it means updating all of them.
RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY=
